<p>Recently, I had a lovely (but unusual) experience at work: a student came up to me and enthused about the topic I had lectured on. You would think (and I would hope) that this kind of experience would be more common, but it isn’t. It made my week, though. I <em>love</em> enthusiasm.</p><p>This student was knowledgeable and very well-read in the topic they were talking about, and practically bursting with excitement to talk about it with someone else. They kept stopping themselves and apologising for ‘going on’ about it. I tried to make it as clear as I possibly could that there was absolutely no need to apologise, and that it’s wonderful when students are interested in, and want to discuss, the topics we teach and work on.</p><p>Why is it such a rare experience? I’ve checked with colleagues (thinking, of course, that it must be something I was doing wrong), but it seems to be a common experience. I think that students are very wary of expressing an opinion, and terrified of being wrong. There’s that saying that science is a process of being “progressively less wrong”. I have always tried hard to communicate to students that I — like them — am still continually learning, that if they come across an idea or statement that seems incorrect and contradicted by evidence, they shouldn’t assume that <em>they</em> (the student) must be mistaken. Still, they are scared about being wrong.</p><p>Even worse than that, they seem to be reluctant to show enthusiasm. I really hope that’s the explanation, rather than not <em>feeling</em> enthusiasm. That would be heartbreaking. Enthusiasm (both expressing it and experiencing someone enthusing on a topic) is a life-affirming thing. I love it when people are enthusiastic, even if it’s something that I’m not interested in myself. Enthusiasm is infectious, and if it’s something you are super keen on, I’m going to listen and try to learn more about why you find it so fascinating. Maybe I’m missing something (or maybe I’m not), but your attention to the minutiae of a topic is a good signal for me that it might be worth learning about.</p><p>That’s why I love the small web so much: it’s chock full of people being enthusiastic things, and I love to see that.</p>